we would now like to begin our analysis  of motion and we'll start with one  dimensional motion let's just consider a  person running along a road and so  here's our runner and here's our road  and what we want to do is be able to  describe the position the velocity the  acceleration of this runner in order to  do that we need our first mathematical  tool which is a coordinate system what  is a coordinate system  well the first thing that we want to  choose is an origin we can pick the  origin anywhere we want  that's a degree of freedom so let's  choose a point along our road as our  origin the next thing to do is to choose  an axis well the road is naturally  defining an axis for our problem and so  we'll call this the x axis now along  with that axis we want to divide that up  into some type of units so we might  divide our axis like this and what's  important here is to introduce a  positive coordinate direction and so the  way I'll do that is I'll call this the  plus X and on this side of the origin X  is negative now the third most important  thing is that we're going to talk about  vectors in physics so we want a choice  of unit vector now because it's only one  dimensional motion we only need one unit  vector so what that means is a choice of  unit vector at every point let's just  consider a point here p1 so our unit  vector I'll call it I hat 1 now let's  pick another point here we'll call this  the point p2 and over here we'll choose  a unit vector I had to so every single  point in space has a unit vector now  what's unique about this one dimensional  linear partition coordinate system is  the following these unit vectors have  the same magnitude a unit vector by  definition has magnitude 1  and they're pointing in the same  direction so if two vectors have the  same magnitude and point in the same  direction they're equal so what makes  this coordinate system unique this  Cartesian coordinate system that at  every single point no matter where we  are the unit vectors point in the  positive direction because they have the  same magnitude and direction  all of these unit vectors are equal and  we call that I hat so symbolically we  may draw the a picture and indicate our  unit vector in the positive x-direction  and this is our coordinate system and we  can next begin to describe the position  and the velocity of our object as it's  moving along this x direction
 

